Brainstorming – LoRa Module Selection and Additional Emergency Features for Terralink

Current Situation:
  In the current Terralink prototype, we are using the SX1278 LoRa module for communication between the sender, intermediate nodes and receiver. The module was sufficient for developing and testing the basic concept of our project, including sending emergency information and forwarding messages through intermediate nodes.
  Now, as we are planning to move Terralink from a prototype towards a real-world project, we felt that the communication module and the overall usability of the device should be reconsidered. Since the main purpose of Terralink is to provide communication during emergencies in places where mobile networks or the internet may not be available, long-range and reliable communication is one of the most important parts of the project.
  At the same time, we also considered adding a few simple emergency features that can help the user in a real survival situation. These features should be useful, easy to use and should not unnecessarily increase the power consumption of the device.

Our main requirements for the new LoRa module are:
- Long communication range
- Target of at least 10 km under favourable conditions
- Low power consumption
- Suitable for battery-powered devices
- Reliable communication for emergency messages
- Ability to handle network traffic and multiple incoming messages
- Suitable for multi-hop communication
- Compatibility with a properly matched external antenna
- Suitable for the applicable sub-GHz operating band in India


Modules Considered:
Instead of directly replacing SX1278 with any newer module, we considered different LoRa transceivers based on our requirements.

SX1278:
 SX1278 is the module used in our current prototype. It is a proven and widely used LoRa transceiver, but it belongs to an older generation. It is suitable for developing the initial prototype, but for the real-world version, we wanted to explore a more power-efficient option.

SX1262:
 SX1262 is a newer-generation sub-GHz LoRa transceiver. It provides long-range communication with lower power consumption, making it suitable for portable and battery-powered devices.
 It also provides good receiver sensitivity and link budget, which are important for improving communication reliability over longer distances.
 Although there are more advanced LoRa transceivers available, the most advanced module is not always the best choice for a particular project. For the current requirements of Terralink, SX1262 provides the required performance without adding unnecessary complexity.
 The SX1262 is selected as the communication transceiver for each Terralink node, while handling packet collisions, duplicate messages and controlled forwarding will be managed through the Terralink communication protocol.

Communication Range Target:
 For the real-world version of Terralink, our target is:
 To achieve at least 10 km communication under favourable conditions using a properly matched external antenna and suitable antenna placement.
 We understand that 10 km cannot be guaranteed in every environment. The actual range will depend on several factors such as:

- Line of sight
- Terrain
- Buildings and other obstacles
- Dense vegetation or forest
- Antenna type and height
- LoRa spreading factor
- Bandwidth
- Transmit power
- RF interference
Therefore, the range mentioned is a design and testing target, not a fixed range guaranteed by the module alone.


Additional Emergency Features:
 Along with improving the communication system, we also considered adding a few basic features that can be useful when a person is in a remote or emergency situation.
 The selected features are a normal magnetic compass, refillable butane piezo lighter and 1 W high-brightness white LED torch.

Normal Magnetic Compass:
 A normal magnetic compass was selected instead of a digital compass module.
 The purpose of the compass is to help the user identify basic directions such as north, south, east and west. A normal compass is sufficient for this purpose and does not require any electrical power.
The main advantages are:
- No battery or electrical power required
- Low cost
- Simple and easy to use
- Works even when the Terralink device battery is completely discharged
- Provides an independent backup for basic navigation
The compass should be placed away from the battery, high-current wiring and other components that may affect its magnetic needle.


Refillable Butane Piezo Lighter:
 A refillable butane piezo lighter is included as a basic emergency survival feature, mainly to help the user start a fire in cold conditions.
 It does not consume power from the Terralink battery and does not require charging. The piezoelectric ignition makes it easy to use, while the refillable design allows it to be reused.
The main advantages are:
- No power consumption from the Terralink device
- Easy to use
- Compact
- Refillable and reusable
- Useful for starting a fire in cold conditions
For safety, the lighter will be placed in a separate and physically isolated compartment from the battery, PCB and other electronic components. This will reduce the risk of the flammable fuel coming into contact with the electronics.


1 W High-Brightness White LED Torch:
 A 1 W high-brightness white LED torch is selected to provide illumination during emergencies and low-light conditions.
 The torch can help the user navigate in darkness, search for a safe path or attract the attention of nearby rescuers.
 A 1 W LED was selected because Terralink is primarily an emergency communication device, and the torch should provide useful illumination without placing excessive load on the battery.

The torch can also support different modes such as:
- Low brightness mode for saving power
- High brightness mode for normal use
- SOS flashing mode for emergency signaling
The LED should be used with a suitable driver circuit and a separate control button so that the torch can be operated without affecting the communication system.


Proposed Communication Structure:
 The basic Terralink communication structure will remain the same:
 Sender Node → Intermediate Node(s) → Receiver

Sender Node:
The sender will collect the emergency information such as:
- SOS status
- Sender ID
- GPS location
- Battery information
The data will then be sent through the SX1262 LoRa module.

Intermediate Node:
The intermediate node will:
- Receive the emergency packet
- Check the Packet ID and Sender ID
- Check whether it is a duplicate packet
- Check the hop count
- Wait for a controlled or random delay when required
- Forward the packet to the next available node

Receiver Node:
The receiver will receive and display the emergency information, including the sender details and location.


Proposed Field Testing:
Before finalizing the SX1262 hardware, practical testing should be carried out in different environments:

1. Open Area
 To test the maximum communication range and check the 10 km target.

2. Semi-Urban Area
 To understand the effect of buildings and other obstacles.

3. Forest or Dense Vegetation
 To test the system in one of the possible real-world emergency environments.

4. Hilly or Uneven Terrain
 To understand the effect of terrain on communication.

5. Multiple Node Test
 Multiple Terralink nodes should transmit messages to test how the system handles network traffic and possible packet collisions.

For this test, we should observe what happens when several nodes transmit at nearly the same time and evaluate the effectiveness of the controlled forwarding and duplicate detection methods.
The additional emergency features should also be checked during practical testing to ensure that they are properly integrated and do not affect the main communication system.


Final Decision:
After considering the available LoRa options, we have finalized the SX1262-based LoRa module for the real-world version of Terralink.
SX1262 was selected because it provides a practical balance between long-range communication, low power consumption, reliability and implementation complexity.
The real-world version of Terralink will also include the following basic emergency features:
- Normal magnetic compass for basic direction finding without power consumption
- Refillable butane piezo lighter in a separate compartment for emergency fire starting without using the device battery
- 1 W high-brightness white LED torch for illumination and visual emergency signaling

The final system will use an SX1262 module suitable for the selected operating band in India along with a properly matched external antenna.
In addition to range, the Terralink communication system will also focus on handling multiple incoming messages, reducing packet collisions, preventing duplicate forwarding and managing network traffic.
Our target is to achieve at least 10 km communication under favourable conditions. The final range, reliability and multiple-node communication performance will be confirmed through practical field testing.
